{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When you acquire a Hebrew slave.</b> He began the first legal ruling with the Hebrew slave, because the sending forth of the slave in the seventh year contains a remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt, which is mentioned in the first commandment, as it says concerning it (Deuteronomy 15:15): \"And you shall remember that you were a slave in the land of Egypt, and YHWH your Elohim redeemed you; therefore I command you this thing today.\" And it also contains a remembrance of the work of Creation like the Sabbath, for the seventh year for the slave is a rest from his master's labor like the seventh day. And it also contains a seventh of years, which is the Jubilee, for the seventh was chosen in days and in years and in the sabbatical years, and all for one matter, and it is the secret of the days of the world from בראשית (Genesis 1:1) until ויכולו (ibid. 2:1). Therefore this commandment is fitting to be placed first, for it is very distinguished, alluding to great matters in the work of Creation. And therefore the prophet was exceedingly strict concerning it, and said: \"I made a covenant with your fathers at the end of seven years: you shall send every man his slave and every man his maidservant\" (Jeremiah 34:13–14), and he decreed exile on its account (ibid. 17–22), just as the Torah decrees exile for the sabbatical rest of the land (Leviticus 26:34–35), as I shall yet write with the help of HaShem (in Leviticus 25:2). And when He completed the legal ruling of this commandment concerning Hebrew slaves, He began with the law of לא תרצח (you shall not murder), which is the most severe, and with the honor of fathers, and with לא תגנוב (you shall not steal), and then returned to the law of one who strikes a man without his dying from it, and after that to the murder of a slave, which is more severe than the killing of the unborn, and after that to the limbs of the Israelites and of the slaves, and after that to damages involving livestock, in cases of death. And all the sections are arranged with order and correct arrangement."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {0 "Ramban's phrase 'במיתה' at the end of the passage — 'נזקי הבהמות במיתה' — likely refers to cases where the beast kills (e.g., the goring ox), linking to the next section of laws; rendered hyper-literally as 'in cases of death.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:17:41.234826023Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}